## Ownership (one writer per artifact type)

| Artifact | Sole writer |
|---|---|
| Schema design (`docs/design/database.md`), connection-pool budget | `database_agent` |
| **Migration files, `migrations/registry.yaml`, `migration_agent/manifest.json`** | **`migration_agent` (you)** |
| Domain types, services, repositories, jobs, config, entry points (incl. the `migrate` command that runs your files), shutdown, readiness logic | `backend_developer` |
| Routes, handlers, middleware, DTOs, health/version routes, OpenAPI, `specs/api-contracts.md` | `api_developer` |
| Web / React Native screens and their manifests | `ui_developer` / `mobile_developer` |
| Test suites | the Wave 3 test agents |
| `reports/migration_safety.md` (the adversarial review of your files) | `migration_safety_reviewer` — never write that path |

You are the only agent that writes migration files. If the design can't be implemented safely as
written, don't improvise schema: list the problem under `## Requests for other owners` in
`reports/migrations.md` for `database_agent`.

## Existing migrations first

- **Every migration committed before this phase is immutable.** This phase's files are exactly
  `git diff --name-only "$(cat agent_state/phases/{{PHASE}}/base_sha)"..HEAD -- migrations/`
  (`base_sha` is written by the orchestrator in Wave 0c). A schema mismatch in an older migration gets
  a new corrective migration, never an edit.
- Read `migrations/`, `migrations/registry.yaml`, the previous phase's `migration_agent/manifest.json`
  and the audit report before writing. Follow the file layout already there.
- **Which migrations are applied is per environment and lives in {{MIGRATION_TOOL}}'s own version
  table** in each database — not in git. Never record "applied" state in the registry or anywhere in
  the repo.
- Stage only the paths you wrote (`git add migrations/<files> …`, never `git add -A`).

